export const PASSAGES = [{"id":"g12-u1-p01","unit":"G12-U1","pool":"practice","title":"Ending the Legacy Advantage","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every spring, thousands of high school seniors open admissions decisions from selective colleges, and a small share of them carry an advantage they did nothing to earn: a parent or grandparent who attended the same school. These \"legacy preferences\" have shaped American higher education for roughly a century, and they deserve to end. A college that claims to reward talent and effort cannot credibly keep a thumb on the scale for applicants whose main distinction is their family tree.\n\nThe first reason is fairness. Admissions officers routinely describe their work as a search for promise, and applicants are told that grades, essays, and accomplishments decide the outcome. Yet when a school quietly adds weight for family connections, it rewards an accident of birth. Because the parents of today's legacy applicants attended selective colleges decades ago, when those campuses were far less diverse than they are now, the preference tends to benefit students who are already wealthier and more likely to be white than the applicant pool as a whole. A policy that compounds old advantages is difficult to defend in the language of merit.\n\nThe second reason is that the preference is unnecessary. Defenders argue that legacy admissions keep alumni loyal and donations flowing, but the experience of colleges that have abandoned the practice suggests otherwise. Johns Hopkins University stopped considering legacy status in 2014, and Amherst College ended the practice in 2021; both remain wealthy, selective, and well supported by their graduates. In 2024, California went further and passed a law barring legacy and donor preferences at its private colleges. If the policy were truly essential to an institution's survival, these schools would by now offer evidence of decline. Instead, they offer evidence that loyalty can be built on pride in a school's mission rather than on the promise of a reserved seat for the next generation.\n\nA thoughtful opponent might raise a stronger objection. Legacy status, the argument goes, is only one factor among many, and most legacy applicants are highly qualified anyway; eliminating the preference would change very little while insulting families who have supported a school for generations. This objection deserves a serious answer, because it is partly true. Many legacy applicants are excellent students. But if the preference changes very little, then removing it costs very little, and the symbolic gain is considerable. A college that ends the practice tells every applicant, including the first person in a family to apply anywhere, that the competition is genuinely open. Moreover, at schools that admit only a small percentage of applicants, even a modest advantage can decide who receives one of a limited number of places.\n\nFinally, ending legacy preferences would strengthen public trust in higher education at a time when that trust is fragile. National surveys have recorded declining confidence in colleges among American adults, and highly publicized admissions scandals have reinforced the suspicion that the system is rigged for insiders. Colleges cannot repair every inequality that shapes an applicant's life before senior year. They can, however, stop adding one of their own.\n\nIt is worth asking, too, what message the preference sends to current students. A campus that admits some applicants partly because of their surnames invites quiet doubts about who belongs there and why. Students who earned their places without any such help may wonder whether their classmates did the same, and legacy students themselves may carry an unfair suspicion that their achievements were purchased by their parents' diplomas. Removing the preference protects everyone from that corrosive uncertainty.\n\nThe case, then, rests on three connected claims: the preference contradicts the stated values of admissions, it is not needed to sustain institutions, and abandoning it would rebuild credibility. None of these claims requires colleges to pretend that families do not exist; an applicant may still write movingly about what a parent's education meant to her. What colleges should surrender is the automatic advantage. Merit is a promise, and institutions that make that promise should keep it.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"What is the author's central claim?","options":["Colleges should stop favoring applicants whose relatives attended them.","Colleges should rely mainly on grades when choosing new students.","Colleges should require alumni donations before admitting their children."],"answer":0},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author answer the objection that legacy status changes very little?","options":["By calling the objection entirely false and moving on","By arguing that a small effect is also cheap to remove","By showing that most legacy applicants are weak students"],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that colleges can thrive without legacy preferences?","options":["Because the parents of today's legacy applicants attended selective colleges decades ago, when those campuses were far less diverse than they are now, the preference tends to benefit students who are already wealthier and more likely to be white than the applicant pool as a whole.","Johns Hopkins University stopped considering legacy status in 2014, and Amherst College ended the practice in 2021; both remain wealthy, selective, and well supported by their graduates.","Colleges cannot repair every inequality that shapes an applicant's life before senior year."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Johns Hopkins University stopped considering legacy status in 2014, and Amherst College ended the practice in 2021; both remain wealthy, selective, and well supported by their graduates."},{"type":"vocab","q":"As used in the second paragraph, what does \"compounds\" most nearly mean?","options":["builds on and enlarges","breaks into small parts","hides from public view"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A policy that compounds old advantages is difficult to defend in the language of merit."}],"grade":12,"words":661,"auto":{"fk":12.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u1-p02","unit":"G12-U1","pool":"practice","title":"Bringing the Benin Bronzes Home","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In February 1897, a British military expedition captured Benin City, in what is now southern Nigeria, and removed thousands of objects from the royal palace: cast brass plaques, carved ivory tusks, and commemorative heads that had recorded the history of the kingdom for centuries. The objects, known collectively as the Benin Bronzes, were sold and scattered among museums and private collectors in Europe and North America. For more than a hundred years, visitors in London, Berlin, and New York admired them behind glass, often with little explanation of how they had arrived. Museums that still hold objects taken in this way should return them, and the recent history of the Bronzes shows that they can.\n\nThe central reason is a principle that most people accept in every other context: property taken by force does not become legitimately owned simply because time has passed. If a painting stolen from a family during the Second World War surfaces in a gallery, we expect it to be returned to the heirs, and many museums have done exactly that. The Benin Bronzes were not purchased, traded, or given. They were seized as spoils after a military assault. A museum that would never knowingly display a stolen painting should apply the same standard to a stolen plaque, regardless of how long the plaque has hung on its walls.\n\nA second reason is practical: return is no longer hypothetical. In 2022, Germany signed an agreement transferring ownership of more than a thousand Benin objects in its public collections to Nigeria. That same year, the Smithsonian's National Museum of African Art returned twenty-nine pieces, and the Horniman Museum in London transferred ownership of seventy-two objects. These institutions did not collapse, and their visitors did not lose access to African art. Several of them arranged long-term loans, so that some objects remain on display abroad under a new and honest understanding of who owns them.\n\nThe strongest objection comes from the idea of the \"universal museum.\" According to this view, great collections in major cities allow millions of people to compare the achievements of many cultures side by side, and dispersing those collections would shrink the world's shared heritage. Defenders also worry about a slippery slope: if the Bronzes go back, what will remain in any museum?\n\nThese concerns are sincere, but they do not survive close examination. Ownership and display are separate questions. A museum can borrow objects, partner with institutions in the countries of origin, and exhibit them with full context, all without claiming title to property taken by force. The slippery slope is also less steep than it appears. The principle defended here applies to objects removed through conquest or theft, not to everything acquired abroad; a vase bought openly from a willing seller raises a different question entirely. Drawing that distinction requires careful research into each object's history, but museums already employ specialists whose job is to trace exactly such histories.\n\nFinally, the universal museum argument has an awkward feature: the universe it imagines is usually located in a handful of wealthy capitals. People in Lagos or Benin City who wish to see the art made by their own ancestors have often needed a passport and an expensive plane ticket. A truly universal approach to heritage would make these objects accessible where their meaning is deepest, while sharing them with the rest of the world through loans and exhibitions. In 2023, the Nigerian government formally recognized the Oba of Benin, the traditional ruler descended from the king whose palace was looted in 1897, as the owner of the returned objects, a decision that places them back within the living institution that commissioned many of them.\n\nThe British Museum still holds more than nine hundred objects from Benin, and it has argued that British law limits its ability to give away its collection. That obstacle is real, but laws are written by people and can be revised by them. The question is not whether returning looted objects is possible, since others have already done it, and done it without diminishing their own standing as centers of learning. The question is whether the institutions that still hold them are willing to act on the principle they claim to honor.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"Which statement best expresses the counterclaim the author addresses?","options":["Major museums let many people compare world cultures in one place.","The Benin Bronzes were purchased fairly from willing sellers.","Nigeria has refused every offer to take the objects back."],"answer":0},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author mention a painting stolen during the Second World War?","options":["To show that museums once collected art carelessly","To compare the Bronzes to a case most readers already judge","To argue that European art deserves more protection"],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"What did Germany agree to do in 2022?","options":["Buy back Benin objects from private collectors","Transfer ownership of more than a thousand objects","Lend its Benin objects to Nigerian museums"],"answer":1,"evidence":"In 2022, Germany signed an agreement transferring ownership of more than a thousand Benin objects in its public collections to Nigeria."},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author limit the principle in order to answer the slippery-slope worry?","options":["By applying it only to objects taken through conquest or theft","By applying it only to objects made of brass and ivory","By applying it only to museums located in Germany"],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":706,"auto":{"fk":12.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u1-c01","unit":"G12-U1","pool":"check","title":"Let Workers Walk Out the Door","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"When a worker signs a noncompete agreement, she promises that if she leaves her job, she will not work for a competitor, or start a competing business, for a set period of time and within a set area. Such clauses once belonged mostly to senior executives who carried trade secrets in their heads. They no longer do. Federal regulators estimated in 2023 that roughly one in five American workers, about thirty million people, was bound by a noncompete, including hair stylists, security guards, nurses and warehouse supervisors. A national sandwich chain once required its shop employees to promise not to work for nearby rivals after quitting. Clauses like these do far more harm than good, and states should ban noncompete agreements for ordinary employees.\n\nThe first reason is that noncompetes weaken a worker's most basic bargaining tool: the ability to leave. In a healthy labor market, an employer who pays poorly loses people to employers who pay better, and that threat pushes wages up. A noncompete removes the threat. A dental hygienist who cannot work at another practice within twenty miles for two years has two realistic choices, accept whatever her current employer offers or abandon her field. That is not a fair negotiation, and employers know it.\n\nThe evidence supports this concern. When Oregon banned noncompetes for hourly workers in 2008, economists who later studied the change found that hourly wages in the state rose by roughly two to three percent compared with similar workers elsewhere. Workers in the state also changed jobs more often. Research on other states that limited the clauses has found similar patterns, especially for lower-paid workers. These are not enormous effects, but they are gains that cost taxpayers nothing.\n\nThe second reason is that noncompetes slow the spread of ideas and the creation of new firms. California has refused to enforce noncompete agreements since the nineteenth century, and many scholars argue that this rule helped Silicon Valley grow. Engineers moved freely between companies, carried skills with them and founded startups when they saw an opportunity their employers had missed. A region where talent circulates learns faster than a region where talent is locked in place.\n\nDefenders of noncompetes make a serious argument that deserves an answer. Employers, they say, invest heavily in training, and they share confidential information such as client lists, pricing strategies and product plans. Without a noncompete, a rival could simply hire away a trained employee and capture that investment for free. Some business groups also warn that a ban would make companies more reluctant to train anyone at all.\n\nThis argument is real, but it proves less than it claims. Employers already have narrower tools that protect what they legitimately own. Trade-secret law punishes anyone who takes confidential formulas or data. Nondisclosure agreements forbid workers from sharing specific information. Nonsolicitation clauses can stop a departing salesperson from taking her old clients with her. Employers can also keep trained workers the way every other industry does, by paying them well enough that they choose to stay. None of these tools requires preventing a person from earning a living in her own profession.\n\nMoreover, the training argument hardly fits the jobs where noncompetes have spread fastest. A sandwich maker or a fitness instructor does not carry secrets that could ruin a former employer. In those cases, the clause protects nothing except the employer's ability to keep wages low and turnover rare.\n\nThe federal government tried to settle this question in 2024, when the Federal Trade Commission adopted a rule banning most noncompetes nationwide. A federal court in Texas blocked the rule before it took effect, and the agency later abandoned its defense of it. The question has therefore returned to the states, several of which, including Minnesota in 2023, have already passed broad bans of their own.\n\nStates should follow their lead. A sensible law would void noncompete agreements for nearly all workers while preserving trade-secret protections and allowing limited agreements when someone sells a business she owns. Such a law would not end competition between employers. It would restore it.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"What is the author's central claim?","options":["States should ban noncompete agreements for most ordinary workers.","Noncompetes should apply only to workers in the technology industry.","The federal government should write all rules about employment."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that banning noncompetes can raise pay?","options":["When Oregon banned noncompetes for hourly workers in 2008, economists who later studied the change found that hourly wages in the state rose by roughly two to three percent compared with similar workers elsewhere.","Federal regulators estimated in 2023 that roughly one in five American workers, about thirty million people, was bound by a noncompete, including hair stylists, security guards, nurses and warehouse supervisors.","A sensible law would void noncompete agreements for nearly all workers while preserving trade-secret protections and allowing limited agreements when someone sells a business she owns."],"answer":0,"evidence":"When Oregon banned noncompetes for hourly workers in 2008, economists who later studied the change found that hourly wages in the state rose by roughly two to three percent compared with similar workers elsewhere."},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author respond to the counterclaim about training costs?","options":["By arguing that other legal tools already protect employers' real interests","By admitting that employers should be allowed any agreement they choose","By claiming that companies never spend money on training their workers"],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the author mention the sandwich chain in two places?","options":["To show that noncompetes now reach jobs that hold no real secrets","To praise a company that trains its workers better than its rivals","To prove that the Texas court decision was mistaken about the law"],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the final paragraph, what does the word \"void\" mean?","options":["Cancel so it has no legal force","Shorten to a smaller time period","Review again in a higher court"],"answer":0,"evidence":"A sensible law would void noncompete agreements for nearly all workers while preserving trade-secret protections and allowing limited agreements when someone sells a business she owns."}],"grade":12,"words":682,"auto":{"fk":10,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u1-c02","unit":"G12-U1","pool":"check","title":"The Trouble with a Required Year","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every few years, a proposal returns to American public debate: require every young person, at around age eighteen, to spend a year in national service. Participants might tutor children, restore trails, assist in hospitals, respond to floods or enlist in the military. Supporters describe the idea with real warmth. A shared year of service, they argue, would mix young people from different regions, classes and political backgrounds, teach practical skills, and rebuild a sense of common citizenship in a divided country. These goals are admirable. A mandatory year, however, is the wrong way to reach them, and the country would gain more by expanding voluntary service instead.\n\nConsider first the scale of the proposal. Roughly four million Americans turn eighteen each year. AmeriCorps, the main federal civilian service program, typically enrolls a small fraction of that number, and its programs often struggle to find enough well-supervised placements. A mandatory program would need to create millions of meaningful positions every year, along with housing, transportation, training and supervisors for each one. Rough estimates of the cost run into tens of billions of dollars annually. If the placements are poorly designed, the country will have spent a fortune to give young people the experience of waiting around with nothing important to do, which is a poor lesson in citizenship.\n\nCompulsion also changes the meaning of service itself. Much of the power of volunteering comes from the fact that it is chosen. A young woman who decides to spend a year teaching reading in a rural school brings commitment to the work, and her students can tell. A young man ordered to fill the same position may resent it, and resentful helpers rarely help much. Supporters point to countries such as Switzerland, Norway and South Korea, which require military service, but those systems grew out of specific security needs and long national traditions. They are not evidence that a large, diverse country can successfully draft its whole population into civilian work.\n\nThere are fairness problems as well. A required year would delay college and paid employment for every young person, but the burden would not fall equally. A student from a wealthy family can absorb a year without income. A young person who supports a parent, cares for siblings or is already raising a child cannot easily do so. Any workable program would need exemptions, and history suggests that exemptions tend to be easiest to obtain for those with money, connections and good lawyers. The result could deepen the very divisions the program was meant to heal. There is also a legal question. A requirement to perform civilian labor for the government, outside the military, would almost certainly face challenges under the Thirteenth Amendment, which forbids involuntary servitude except as punishment for a crime. Courts have upheld the military draft, but they have never approved a universal civilian labor requirement, and a program built on uncertain legal ground could spend years tied up in litigation.\n\nSupporters have a strong reply. Voluntary programs, they note, mostly attract young people who are already civic-minded, so they rarely produce the mixing of strangers that makes national service attractive. A wealthy suburban graduate and a rural graduate from a struggling town may never meet unless both are required to show up. This is the best argument for compulsion, and it should not be dismissed.\n\nYet the goal of mixing can be pursued without forcing anyone. Congress could fund enough paid service positions that every young person who wants one could have one. It could make service far more attractive by offering generous education awards, student-loan relief or credit toward public-sector jobs. Programs could recruit deliberately across regions and income levels, placing participants in teams with people unlike themselves. These steps would cost money too, but they would spend it on young people who want to be there.\n\nThe appeal of a mandatory year lies in its simplicity: one rule, applied to everyone, producing one shared experience. Real service is rarely that simple. It depends on good placements, skilled supervision and participants who believe in the work. The country should make service a widely available opportunity rather than an obligation.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"Which statement best expresses the author's claim?","options":["Expanded voluntary service would serve the country better than a mandatory year.","Young people should be required to join the military for one year at eighteen.","National service programs should be closed because they cost too much money."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the author's concern about the program's size?","options":["A mandatory program would need to create millions of meaningful positions every year, along with housing, transportation, training and supervisors for each one.","Supporters point to countries such as Switzerland, Norway and South Korea, which require military service, but those systems grew out of specific security needs and long national traditions.","Any workable program would need exemptions, and history suggests that exemptions tend to be easiest to obtain for those with money, connections and good lawyers."],"answer":0,"evidence":"A mandatory program would need to create millions of meaningful positions every year, along with housing, transportation, training and supervisors for each one."},{"type":"claim","q":"Which counterclaim does the author call the strongest argument for compulsion?","options":["Voluntary programs rarely bring together young people from different backgrounds.","Mandatory service would cost far less than any voluntary program could.","Most young people already want to spend a year in military service."],"answer":0},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author discuss Switzerland, Norway and South Korea?","options":["To question whether their systems prove a draft for civilian work would succeed","To argue that the United States should copy their military service rules","To show that those countries have abandoned national service entirely"],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"What does the author suggest about exemptions in a required program?","options":["They could favor families with money and influence.","They would be impossible to offer to anyone at all.","They would mostly benefit young people in rural towns."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":696,"auto":{"fk":10.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u1-c03","unit":"G12-U1","pool":"check","title":"Judges Should Not Campaign","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In most democracies, the idea of a judge running for office would seem strange. Judges are supposed to apply the law impartially, even when the law produces an unpopular result. Yet in the United States, most states choose at least some of their judges through elections, and in more than thirty states voters elect the justices of the state supreme court. These courts decide the great majority of American legal cases, from contract disputes to criminal appeals. States should stop electing judges in contested races and adopt a merit-selection system instead.\n\nThe strongest reason is money. Judicial campaigns were once quiet affairs, but over the past few decades they have become expensive contests financed by lawyers, businesses, unions and political groups, many of whom will later appear before the winning candidate. In 2023, a single election for a seat on the Wisconsin Supreme Court cost more than fifty million dollars, a national record for a judicial race. When a judge's career depends on donors, every citizen who loses a case to a donor has reason to wonder whether the outcome was fair.\n\nThat worry is not hypothetical. In 2004, the chief executive of a coal company spent about three million dollars supporting a candidate for the West Virginia Supreme Court of Appeals while his company was appealing a large jury verdict against it. The candidate won, refused to step aside, and then voted with the majority to overturn the verdict. In Caperton v. A. T. Massey Coal Company, decided in 2009, the United States Supreme Court ruled that the justice's participation created such a serious risk of bias that it violated the other side's right to due process. The case was extreme, but it revealed a structural danger present in every contested judicial race.\n\nElections also pressure judges to rule in ways that will look good in campaign advertisements. Several studies have found that elected trial judges tend to impose longer criminal sentences as their elections approach. One well-known analysis of Pennsylvania courts found that sentences grew harsher as judges neared a retention vote, even after accounting for the seriousness of the crimes. The judges were not necessarily acting in bad faith. They were responding, perhaps without noticing, to the incentives that elections create. A judge who fears an attack ad accusing her of being soft on crime may hesitate to follow the law when the law favors an unpopular defendant. Courts exist precisely to protect rights when the majority would prefer to ignore them, and campaign pressure undermines that role.\n\nDefenders of judicial elections raise an important objection. Judges, they point out, are powerful officials who interpret constitutions and shape public policy. Removing them from the ballot gives that power to governors, bar associations or commissions that voters cannot directly hold accountable. Appointment systems have their own politics, and a governor may simply reward allies.\n\nThis objection deserves weight, but a well-designed merit system answers much of it. Under the approach known as the Missouri Plan, first adopted by Missouri in 1940, a nonpartisan commission of lawyers and citizens screens applicants and sends a short list of qualified candidates to the governor, who must choose from that list. After a period on the bench, the judge faces a retention election in which voters decide only whether to keep her. Voters therefore keep the final say, but judges do not have to raise campaign money or run against opponents. States can strengthen the system further by publishing the commission's evaluations and including members of the public on the commission.\n\nCritics are right that no selection method is free of politics. The question is which method best protects the courtroom from it. Contested elections invite donors and advertisers into the heart of the judicial process, and they encourage judges to think like candidates. Merit selection with retention votes balances accountability against independence far better.\n\nCitizens should be able to trust that a judge is listening to the arguments in front of her, not to the donors behind her. Ending contested judicial elections is a reasonable step toward that trust.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"What does the author want states to do?","options":["Replace contested judicial elections with merit selection and retention votes","Let governors appoint judges for life without any review by voters","Hold judicial elections more often so voters can remove bad judges"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that campaign money can threaten fair rulings?","options":["The candidate won, refused to step aside, and then voted with the majority to overturn the verdict.","These courts decide the great majority of American legal cases, from contract disputes to criminal appeals.","Critics are right that no selection method is free of politics."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The candidate won, refused to step aside, and then voted with the majority to overturn the verdict."},{"type":"claim","q":"Which counterclaim does the author answer?","options":["Removing judges from the ballot reduces voters' ability to hold them accountable.","Judicial campaigns cost too little money to attract serious candidates.","State courts decide too few cases to matter in most people's lives."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"Under the Missouri Plan, who first screens the candidates for judge?","options":["A nonpartisan commission of lawyers and citizens","The state legislature and its committee on courts","The governor's political party and its donors"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Under the approach known as the Missouri Plan, first adopted by Missouri in 1940, a nonpartisan commission of lawyers and citizens screens applicants and sends a short list of qualified candidates to the governor, who must choose from that list."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the author mention studies of criminal sentences?","options":["To show that election pressure may change how judges rule","To argue that judges should always give shorter sentences","To prove that appointed judges never make legal mistakes"],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":681,"auto":{"fk":10.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u3-p01","unit":"G12-U3","pool":"practice","title":"The Chemist Who Counted Lead","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In the late 1940s, a young chemist named Clair Patterson was given what seemed like a narrow technical assignment. Working at the University of Chicago under the geochemist Harrison Brown, he was asked to measure tiny amounts of lead in ancient minerals. The goal was ambitious. Uranium slowly decays into lead at a known rate, so if scientists could measure the proportions of different lead isotopes precisely enough, they could calculate how long the decay had been going on. Brown believed that the right samples, analyzed carefully, could reveal the age of the Earth itself.\n\nThe measurements proved maddeningly difficult. Patterson kept finding far more lead in his samples than he expected, and the numbers refused to agree. Gradually he realized that the problem was not in the rocks. Lead was everywhere in the laboratory: in the dust on the benches, in the glassware, in the air drifting through the windows and even on the scientists' hands. The contamination was so pervasive that it overwhelmed the faint natural signal he was trying to detect.\n\nHis response defined his career. After moving to the California Institute of Technology in 1952, Patterson built one of the first ultraclean laboratories in the world. He filtered the incoming air, sealed the room, scrubbed every surface with acid and required anyone entering to wear protective clothing. Only under such conditions could he trust a measurement. He then turned to meteorites, which formed at about the same time as the Earth and had preserved their original lead. Using material from a meteorite that had struck Arizona thousands of years earlier, he arrived at an age for the Earth of about 4.55 billion years and published it in 1956. That figure has held up, with only minor refinement, for nearly seventy years.\n\nA lesser scientist might have stopped there. Patterson instead asked a troubling question. If ordinary laboratories were so saturated with lead, where had all of it come from? Lead does occur naturally, but he suspected that human activity had raised its level far beyond anything the planet had known. The most likely source was tetraethyl lead, a compound added to gasoline since the 1920s to stop engines from knocking. Every car on the road was releasing fine particles of lead into the air.\n\nPatterson began measuring lead in places where its history could be traced, including deep ocean water and the bones of people buried long before modern industry. His results suggested that the average modern American carried many times more lead than people in earlier centuries. In 1965, he published a paper warning that industrial lead posed a serious threat to public health. The response was hostile. The lead industry and its scientific allies insisted that the levels in the environment were natural and harmless. Patterson later reported that some of his research contracts were not renewed, and in 1971 he was left off a national scientific panel studying lead pollution, even though he was among the leading experts in the field.\n\nOver the following decades, however, the evidence turned decisively in his favor. Other researchers found that even modest exposure to lead could impair children's mental development. The federal government began phasing lead out of gasoline in the 1970s, and leaded fuel was banned for ordinary cars in the United States in 1996. Studies have since recorded dramatic declines in the amount of lead in Americans' blood, one of the great public-health achievements of the twentieth century.\n\nPatterson's story is often told as two separate discoveries, one about geology and one about pollution. In truth, they were the same discovery seen from two sides. The obsessive care that allowed him to measure the age of the planet also allowed him to notice that the planet's surface had been changed. His refusal to accept contaminated data, first in his laboratory and then in public debate, turned a dating problem into a warning that protected millions of people.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?","options":["Patterson's careful methods led him to date the Earth and expose lead pollution.","Patterson proved that meteorites are much younger than the planet they strike.","Patterson's work on lead was quickly accepted by industry and government."],"answer":0},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Patterson do after he moved to Caltech in 1952?","options":["He built one of the first ultraclean laboratories in the world.","He began studying under the geochemist Harrison Brown in Chicago.","He served on the national panel studying lead pollution in 1971."],"answer":0,"evidence":"After moving to the California Institute of Technology in 1952, Patterson built one of the first ultraclean laboratories in the world."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Patterson use meteorites to calculate the Earth's age?","options":["They formed when the Earth did and kept their original lead.","They contained more uranium than any rock found on the Earth.","They were free of lead, so they gave a perfectly clean baseline."],"answer":0},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author say the two discoveries were \"the same discovery seen from two sides\"?","options":["To show that one habit of careful measurement produced both results","To suggest that Patterson copied the pollution work of other scientists","To argue that the age of the Earth matters more than public health"],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":654,"auto":{"fk":10,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u3-p02","unit":"G12-U3","pool":"practice","title":"A General's Warning","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"On the evening of January 17, 1961, three days before he left office, President Dwight D. Eisenhower spoke to the nation on television and radio from the White House. Farewell speeches are usually gracious and forgettable. This one became among the most quoted addresses of the twentieth century, largely because of a single phrase. Eisenhower warned Americans to guard against the influence of what he called the \"military-industrial complex.\"\n\nThe warning carried unusual weight because of who delivered it. Eisenhower was not a pacifist or a critic of the armed forces. He was a career soldier who had commanded the Allied invasion of Normandy in 1944 and had risen to the rank of five-star general. As president during the early Cold War, he had overseen a large expansion of nuclear weapons and supported a strong defense. When a man with that history cautioned the public about the power of the military establishment, listeners could not easily dismiss him as naive.\n\nThe heart of the speech was a historical argument. Until the Second World War, Eisenhower observed, the United States had no permanent arms industry. In earlier conflicts, factories that made farm equipment and household goods had converted to weapons production when war came and converted back when it ended. The Cold War had changed that pattern. The country now maintained an enormous standing military and a large, permanent industry devoted to building its weapons. Millions of Americans worked directly in defense, and the nation spent more each year on military security than the combined net income of all its corporations.\n\nEisenhower did not call this arrangement illegitimate. He described it as a necessity in a dangerous world. His point was that something so large and so new was bound to shape the country's economic, political and even spiritual life in ways Americans had not yet fully grasped. The combination of military leaders, defense contractors and the officials who funded them could develop interests of its own. It might push for weapons and spending that served its members rather than the nation. In the councils of government, he said, citizens must guard against \"unwarranted influence, whether sought or unsought,\" by this complex. The phrase \"sought or unsought\" was important. Eisenhower was not accusing anyone of a conspiracy. He was describing a structural danger that could grow even if every participant acted in good faith.\n\nHe paired this warning with a second, often overlooked one. Scientific research, he noted, was increasingly funded by the federal government and conducted in large, expensive laboratories. He worried that independent inquiry could be crowded out by government contracts, and that public policy could become captive to a narrow technical elite. Both warnings expressed the same concern: that concentrated power, whether military, industrial or scientific, could slip beyond the reach of democratic control.\n\nThe remedy Eisenhower proposed was not a new law or agency. It was citizenship. Only an \"alert and knowledgeable citizenry,\" he argued, could ensure that security and liberty grew together. The phrase placed the responsibility on ordinary Americans, who would have to understand the defense budget, ask hard questions and insist on balance. Eisenhower also urged restraint in a broader sense, asking the country to avoid living only for the present and spending resources that rightly belonged to future generations.\n\nHistorians have traced how the phrase developed. Eisenhower's speechwriters, including Malcolm Moos and Ralph Williams, worked on drafts for many months, and according to some accounts an earlier version referred to a military-industrial-congressional complex before the reference to Congress was dropped. Whatever the drafting history, the final phrase entered everyday language almost immediately.\n\nIn the decades since, the speech has been invoked by people across the political spectrum, from critics of military spending to opponents of government overreach. Its lasting power comes from its restraint. Eisenhower did not tell Americans to dismantle their defenses. He asked them to remember that a free society must watch even the institutions it depends on most.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the central idea of the passage?","options":["Eisenhower warned that a permanent defense system needed watchful citizens.","Eisenhower argued that the United States should end its arms industry.","Eisenhower's farewell address was mainly a tribute to American scientists."],"answer":0},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Eisenhower's background make his warning more persuasive?","options":["He was a famous general, so he could not be called naive about defense.","He had worked for defense companies, so he knew their secret plans.","He had opposed the military all his life, so his views were consistent."],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"As used in the passage, what does \"structural\" most nearly mean?","options":["Built into the way a system is arranged","Related to the design of tall buildings","Caused by one person's deliberate plan"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He was describing a structural danger that could grow even if every participant acted in good faith."},{"type":"inference","q":"What does the phrase \"alert and knowledgeable citizenry\" suggest about Eisenhower's view?","options":["Ordinary people share responsibility for keeping power in balance.","Only military experts can understand questions about national defense.","Congress alone should decide how much the country spends on weapons."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":662,"auto":{"fk":10.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u3-c01","unit":"G12-U3","pool":"check","title":"How the Rosetta Stone Was Read","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In July 1799, French soldiers rebuilding a fort near the town of Rashid, known to Europeans as Rosetta, on the western edge of the Nile Delta, uncovered a broken slab of dark stone covered in writing. The officer in charge recognized that the inscription might be important and reported the find. The soldiers were part of Napoleon Bonaparte's military campaign in Egypt, which had brought scholars as well as troops. When British forces defeated the French army in Egypt, the stone was handed over under the terms of the surrender in 1801, and it has been displayed at the British Museum since 1802. It is now one of the most visited objects in the world, yet its fame rests not on its beauty but on what it made possible.\n\nThe stone records a decree issued by a council of Egyptian priests in 196 BCE, during the reign of the young king Ptolemy V. The decree itself is a fairly routine document honoring the king. What made it extraordinary was that the same text appeared three times, in three different scripts. At the top was Egyptian hieroglyphic writing, the formal script used on temples and monuments. In the middle was Demotic, a cursive script used for everyday documents. At the bottom was ancient Greek, the language of the ruling dynasty, which scholars in 1799 could read easily. For the first time, Europeans possessed a substantial hieroglyphic text accompanied by a translation they could understand.\n\nThat advantage did not produce a quick solution. For centuries, scholars had assumed that hieroglyphs were purely symbolic, each picture representing an idea rather than a sound. This assumption made decipherment nearly impossible, because it encouraged elaborate guesses about mystical meanings instead of systematic analysis. The hieroglyphic section of the stone was also the most damaged, with much of its top portion missing.\n\nThe first real progress came from the British physician and physicist Thomas Young, who studied the stone in the 1810s. Young focused on the cartouches, the oval rings that enclosed certain groups of signs. He reasoned that they contained royal names, and by comparing them with the name of Ptolemy in the Greek text, he correctly identified the sounds of several signs. Young also recognized that the Demotic script was related to the hieroglyphs. Yet he continued to believe that phonetic signs were used mainly to spell foreign names, such as those of Greek rulers, and that native Egyptian words were written symbolically.\n\nThe decisive breakthrough belonged to the French scholar Jean-François Champollion. Champollion had studied many ancient languages, including Coptic, the late form of the Egyptian language that survived in the liturgy of the Egyptian church. His knowledge of Coptic allowed him to connect the sounds of signs to actual Egyptian words. Working with copies of inscriptions from several monuments, he identified the names of the pharaohs Ramesses and Thutmose, both native Egyptian rulers who had lived long before the Greeks arrived. These names were written partly with sound signs, which proved that phonetic writing was not reserved for foreigners. In September 1822, Champollion announced his results in a letter addressed to the secretary of the French Academy of Inscriptions, and in 1824 he published a fuller explanation of the system.\n\nHis central insight was that hieroglyphic writing combined two kinds of signs. Some represented sounds, much like letters, while others represented whole words or clarified the meaning of the words around them. Once scholars understood this mixed system, texts that had been silent for roughly fourteen centuries began to speak. Temple inscriptions, tomb paintings, and administrative records revealed the names of kings, the details of religious beliefs, and the ordinary concerns of scribes and farmers.\n\nThe story of the Rosetta Stone is often told as the tale of a single key unlocking a single door. The fuller history suggests something more instructive. The stone supplied a crucial opportunity, but decipherment required the gradual abandonment of a mistaken assumption, the contributions of more than one scholar, and knowledge of a living tradition that had preserved the Egyptian language long after its ancient script was forgotten.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?","options":["Reading hieroglyphs required the stone, new reasoning and several scholars.","The Rosetta Stone was valuable mainly as a beautiful work of royal art.","Thomas Young deciphered hieroglyphs alone using only the Greek text."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that an old assumption slowed decipherment?","options":["This assumption made decipherment nearly impossible, because it encouraged elaborate guesses about mystical meanings instead of systematic analysis.","When British forces defeated the French army in Egypt, the stone was handed over under the terms of the surrender in 1801, and it has been displayed at the British Museum since 1802.","The hieroglyphic section of the stone was also the most damaged, with much of its top portion missing."],"answer":0,"evidence":"This assumption made decipherment nearly impossible, because it encouraged elaborate guesses about mystical meanings instead of systematic analysis."},{"type":"why","q":"Why were the names Ramesses and Thutmose important to Champollion's argument?","options":["They were Greek rulers whose names Young had missed.","They were native kings whose names used sound signs.","They were priests who wrote the decree on the stone."],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"Which language helped Champollion link signs to real Egyptian words?","options":["Coptic","Demotic","Greek"],"answer":0,"evidence":"His knowledge of Coptic allowed him to connect the sounds of signs to actual Egyptian words."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author end by questioning the \"single key\" version of the story?","options":["To argue that the stone was less useful than people think","To stress that discovery was gradual and shared","To suggest that Champollion took credit unfairly"],"answer":1}],"grade":12,"words":683,"auto":{"fk":11,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u3-c02","unit":"G12-U3","pool":"check","title":"Washington's Case Against Faction","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In September 1796, readers of a Philadelphia newspaper, the American Daily Advertiser, found an unusual item spread across its pages: a long letter from President George Washington addressed to the people of the United States. Washington announced that he would not seek a third term, and he used the occasion to offer advice to the young nation. The letter was never delivered as a speech. It was published in print and quickly reprinted across the country. Today it is known as Washington's Farewell Address, and it remains one of the most important statements of American political thought.\n\nWashington did not write it alone. Four years earlier, when he had hoped to retire after a single term, he had asked James Madison to help him prepare a farewell message. When he finally decided to step down in 1796, he turned to Alexander Hamilton, who reshaped and expanded the earlier draft. The ideas and the final judgment, however, were Washington's own, and the address reflects concerns he had expressed privately for years.\n\nThe most famous section concerns political parties. By 1796, American politics had divided into two rival camps. The Federalists, associated with Hamilton, favored a strong national government and close commercial ties with Britain. The Democratic-Republicans, led by Thomas Jefferson and Madison, feared concentrated federal power and sympathized with revolutionary France. Disputes over a treaty with Britain had produced fierce newspaper attacks, some aimed at Washington himself. The president believed he was watching a dangerous habit take root, one that could outlast any single dispute and harden into a permanent feature of public life.\n\nIn the address, he warned against what he called \"the baneful effects of the spirit of party.\" Washington's argument was more careful than a simple complaint about disagreement. He accepted that the spirit of party grew naturally out of human passions and could never be entirely eliminated. In monarchies, he suggested, it might even serve a useful purpose by checking a king. In a republic whose government was chosen by the people, however, it posed a special threat, because there was no ruler above the parties to restrain them.\n\nHe traced the danger in several steps. Party spirit encouraged citizens to see their opponents as enemies rather than fellow members of one nation. It distracted the government from its real business and stirred up jealousy and false alarms. It opened the door to foreign influence, since rival nations could cultivate friendly factions and work through them. Most seriously, the bitter alternation of one faction dominating another could exhaust the public, until people sought security in the absolute power of a single individual. In this way, Washington believed, partisan conflict could eventually destroy the liberty it claimed to defend.\n\nHis concern about faction was tied to a broader worry about division. Earlier in the address, he urged Americans not to define themselves by geography, as Northern or Southern, Atlantic or Western, and he warned that ambitious leaders might exaggerate regional differences to gain power. He praised the union of the states as the foundation of the people's safety and prosperity. Later, he applied the same logic to foreign affairs, advising the nation to avoid passionate attachments or hatreds toward other countries and to steer clear of permanent alliances.\n\nWashington's warning did not stop the rise of parties. Within a few years, the contest between Federalists and Democratic-Republicans had become the organizing fact of national politics, and political parties have shaped American government ever since. Some historians argue that he misunderstood the value of organized opposition, which allows voters to choose between clear alternatives and hold leaders accountable.\n\nStill, the address has never lost its audience. Since the nineteenth century, a member of the United States Senate has read it aloud each year near Washington's birthday. Its enduring appeal lies in its central question, which every generation must answer for itself: how can a free people disagree fiercely without coming to regard one another as enemies?","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?","options":["Washington warned that party spirit could weaken a republic and its liberty.","Washington argued that the Federalists should replace all other parties.","Washington's address was written mainly to praise the alliance with France."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Washington saw party conflict as a path to tyranny?","options":["Most seriously, the bitter alternation of one faction dominating another could exhaust the public, until people sought security in the absolute power of a single individual.","Earlier in the address, he urged Americans not to define themselves by geography, as Northern or Southern, Atlantic or Western, and he warned that ambitious leaders might exaggerate regional differences to gain power.","The Federalists, associated with Hamilton, favored a strong national government and close commercial ties with Britain."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Most seriously, the bitter alternation of one faction dominating another could exhaust the public, until people sought security in the absolute power of a single individual."},{"type":"why","q":"According to Washington, why was party spirit especially dangerous in a republic?","options":["No ruler stood above the parties to hold them back.","Kings in republics encouraged parties to grow stronger.","Citizens in republics were not permitted to disagree."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"Whom did Washington first ask to help prepare a farewell message?","options":["James Madison","Thomas Jefferson","Alexander Hamilton"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Four years earlier, when he had hoped to retire after a single term, he had asked James Madison to help him prepare a farewell message."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author include the view of some historians near the end?","options":["To present a balanced judgment of Washington's warning","To prove that Washington's address had no effect at all","To explain how Hamilton changed the final draft's wording"],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":659,"auto":{"fk":11.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u3-c03","unit":"G12-U3","pool":"check","title":"The Rights Added Afterward","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"When the delegates to the Constitutional Convention finished their work in Philadelphia on 17 September 1787, the document they signed said remarkably little about individual rights. It protected the privilege of habeas corpus, which prevents the government from imprisoning people without legal cause, and it banned religious tests for federal office. But it contained no general guarantee of freedom of speech, freedom of the press, or the right to a trial by jury in all cases. Today the first ten amendments, known collectively as the Bill of Rights, are among the most familiar words in American law. Their history shows that they were not part of the original design at all. They were the price of ratification, won through public argument by people who distrusted the new government.\n\nThe omission was noticed immediately. George Mason of Virginia, one of the most respected delegates, refused to sign the Constitution, in part because it lacked a declaration of rights. During the months of debate that followed, opponents of ratification, often called Anti-Federalists, made the missing protections one of their central complaints. They argued that a powerful national government, far from the people it governed, might one day silence its critics or deny accused persons a fair trial. Without written limits, they warned, Americans would have to rely on the goodwill of officials.\n\nSupporters of the Constitution answered that a bill of rights was unnecessary. Alexander Hamilton made the most famous version of this argument in Federalist No. 84, published in 1788. The national government, he wrote, would have only the specific powers granted to it, so there was no need to forbid it from doing things it had no authority to do in the first place. He went further, warning that a list of rights could even be dangerous, because it might imply that any right not listed could be taken away. The argument was clever, but many readers found it unconvincing. Thomas Jefferson, then serving as the American minister to France, wrote to James Madison that a bill of rights is what the people are entitled to against every government on earth.\n\nThe debate shaped the ratification process itself. Several states, including Massachusetts, approved the Constitution while recommending amendments to protect individual liberties. The compromise was implicit but powerful: the Constitution would be accepted now, and its critics expected it to be improved soon.\n\nMadison became the unlikely author of that improvement. He had initially shared Hamilton's view that a bill of rights was of little practical value. But he faced a difficult campaign for a seat in the new House of Representatives, and he promised voters in Virginia that he would support amendments. He also came to believe that written protections could serve a useful purpose, both by educating the public and by giving judges a clear standard. On 8 June 1789, he introduced a series of proposed amendments in the House, drawing heavily on the declarations of rights that several states had already adopted.\n\nCongress debated, revised, and combined Madison's proposals, and in September 1789 it sent twelve amendments to the states. Ten of them were ratified by the required number of states, a process completed when Virginia approved them on 15 December 1791. Those ten became the Bill of Rights. Madison also included a safeguard that answered Hamilton's warning directly. The Ninth Amendment declares that listing certain rights shall not be read to deny or disparage others retained by the people.\n\nOne of the two amendments that failed in 1791 had an unusual afterlife. It required that any change in the pay of members of Congress take effect only after the next election. Largely forgotten for nearly two centuries, it was revived by a campaign begun in the 1980s and finally ratified in 1992 as the Twenty-seventh Amendment.\n\nThe history of the Bill of Rights is therefore a history of persuasion. The protections many Americans now regard as the heart of the Constitution were added because citizens refused to accept a framework of government without them.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What central idea does the author develop throughout the passage?","options":["The Bill of Rights resulted from public pressure during ratification.","The Bill of Rights was drafted in full at the Philadelphia convention.","The Bill of Rights was mainly Hamilton's idea from the beginning."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows how Madison responded to Hamilton's concern that listing rights was dangerous?","options":["He also came to believe that written protections could serve a useful purpose, both by educating the public and by giving judges a clear standard.","The Ninth Amendment declares that listing certain rights shall not be read to deny or disparage others retained by the people.","Congress debated, revised, and combined Madison's proposals, and in September 1789 it sent twelve amendments to the states."],"answer":1,"evidence":"The Ninth Amendment declares that listing certain rights shall not be read to deny or disparage others retained by the people."},{"type":"claim","q":"What was Hamilton's main reason for saying a bill of rights was unnecessary?","options":["The states already had their own courts and juries.","The national government had only its granted powers.","The people would never elect officials who abused power."],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"What does the passage suggest about why Madison changed his position?","options":["Political promises and new reasoning both played a part.","Hamilton personally asked him to draft the amendments.","Courts had ordered Congress to add written protections."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"When was the ratification of the first ten amendments completed?","options":["15 December 1791","17 September 1787","8 June 1789"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Ten of them were ratified by the required number of states, a process completed when Virginia approved them on 15 December 1791."}],"grade":12,"words":670,"auto":{"fk":10.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u5-p01","unit":"G12-U5","pool":"practice","title":"The Bridge Painter's Daughter","genre":"poetry","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"I. Primer\n\nBefore the color, there is the color under color,\na dull red the crew calls primer\nand my mother calls the truth.\nRust, she says, is just the iron going home,\nreturning, molecule by patient molecule,\nto the stone it was dug from.\nHer job is to argue with that homecoming.\nEvery morning she climbs into the girders\nwith a wire brush and a bucket,\nscrapes the flaking scabs of last decade's paint,\nand lays the red down thin and even\nso the steel will stay a bridge\na little longer than it wants to.\n\nII. Harness\n\nShe shows me once, in our kitchen,\nhow the harness buckles: leg loops, chest strap,\nthe lanyard with its steel hook snapping shut\nlike a word you cannot take back.\nTwo points of contact, always, she says.\nOne hand for the bridge and one for you.\nI am nine. I think she is describing climbing.\nYears later I understand\nshe was describing everything:\nhow to hold a job and a daughter,\na marriage and its ending,\nhow to lean far out over a river\nand still be fastened to something.\n\nIII. Color\n\nThe city chose the color by committee:\na green they named for a river bird,\nthough no one on the crew had ever seen one.\nShe brings home the paint chip in her pocket,\nprops it on the windowsill beside the salt,\nand all that summer the kitchen is lit\nby a small green rectangle of argument.\nOn the bridge the color goes on in sections,\none span, then the next, so that for months\nthe old gray and the new green meet midway,\ntwo decades shaking hands above the traffic.\nCommuters honk. She cannot hear them.\nShe hears the brush, the wind in the cables,\nand the river underneath, keeping its own time.\n\nIV. Dinner\n\nShe does not talk about the height.\nShe talks about the pigeons nesting in the bearings,\nthe welder who sings badly,\nthe inspector with a mirror on a stick\nwho checks the hidden joints for cracks.\nShe talks about the bolts, three hundred thousand,\neach one torqued by a human hand.\nWhen I ask if she is ever afraid,\nshe cuts her bread into exact halves\nand says that fear is just attention\nthat has not found its work yet.\nThen she gives me the bigger half.\n\nV. Winter\n\nIn January the crew comes down.\nPaint will not cure below a certain cold,\nso the bridge goes on rusting without her,\nquietly, the way a house ages while you sleep.\nShe is restless all winter. She repairs\nthe hinge on the cabinet, the leg of a chair,\nthe zipper on my coat, the clock in the hall,\nas if the whole apartment were a structure\nwhose joints she had been hired to inspect.\nAt night I hear her at the window,\ncounting the lights strung along the cables,\na necklace laid across the dark throat of the river.\nOnce I find her standing there at two in the morning,\nnot worried, only watching,\nthe way some people watch the sea.\nIt is still there, she tells me,\nas if I had asked.\nAs if anyone could lose a bridge.\n\nVI. Crossing\n\nNow I drive across it twice a day.\nThe green has faded, as she said it would,\nto something nearer to the river's gray,\nand another crew, in other harnesses,\nis scraping it down to the red again.\nI do not slow. You cannot, on a bridge.\nBut at the center span I always look up\nat the riveted lattice, the ordinary miracle\nof weight suspended over moving water,\nand I think: someone held on here.\nSomeone stood in the wind with a brush\nand made the iron wait.\nThe paint is not the bridge, she used to say.\nThe paint is how you tell the bridge\nthat someone is still paying attention.\nI cross. Behind me, the steel holds.\nAhead of me, the steel holds.\nUnder everything, the red.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"What does the mother's comment that rust is \"the iron going home\" suggest?","options":["Decay is a natural return that her work slows but never stops.","The bridge was built from iron mined near the family's house.","Rust makes the bridge stronger by sealing the steel inside it."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"How does section II move beyond its literal subject?","options":["The harness rules become advice about staying connected in life.","The harness is described as a danger the daughter learns to fear.","The harness shows that the mother wishes she had another job."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of closing the sequence with the line \"Under everything, the red\"?","options":["It returns to the primer as a lasting, hidden foundation.","It reveals that the daughter has decided to paint bridges.","It suggests that the bridge is about to collapse into rust."],"answer":0},{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme do the six sections develop together?","options":["Careful, unseen work is a form of care that outlasts the worker.","City committees rarely choose colors that ordinary people like.","Children should never take jobs that put them in physical danger."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":668,"auto":{"fk":5.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u5-p02","unit":"G12-U5","pool":"practice","title":"The Compass and the Flea","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"John Donne, who lived from 1572 to 1631, wrote some of the strangest love poems in the English language, and then some of the most intense religious poems. He was a law student, a soldier on naval expeditions, a secretary to a powerful official and, after a secret marriage ruined his early career, a struggling father of a large family. Late in life he became a priest and eventually dean of Saint Paul's Cathedral in London, where his sermons drew crowds. Most of his poems circulated only in handwritten copies during his lifetime and were first collected in print in 1633, two years after his death. Readers have argued about them ever since.\n\nThe feature that most divides those readers is the conceit, an extended metaphor that links two things so unlike each other that the comparison at first seems absurd. Earlier love poets of the period often compared a beloved to a rose, a star or a summer day. Donne preferred to compare love to a pair of drawing compasses, a map, a flea or a problem in geometry. The surprise is deliberate. A familiar comparison asks the reader to nod; a strange one forces the reader to think, and Donne wanted his readers thinking.\n\nHis most famous conceit appears in a poem in which a speaker, about to leave on a journey, tells the woman he loves not to grieve. Their two souls, he argues, are like \"stiff twin compasses,\" the hinged instrument used to draw circles. She is the fixed foot planted at the center; he is the foot that travels around the edge. When he moves away, she leans after him, and when he returns, she straightens. Her steadiness, he concludes, is what allows his circle to be complete and brings him back to where he began. The comparison is mechanical and almost comically precise, yet it carries real tenderness. It suggests that love can be measured in faithfulness rather than physical closeness.\n\nAnother poem takes the method to an extreme. Its speaker points to a flea that has bitten both him and the woman he is courting, so that their blood is now mingled inside the tiny insect. With mock seriousness, he argues that the flea has become their marriage bed and temple, and that killing it would be a kind of sacrilege. The poem is playful and outrageous, a display of wit in which the argument is more important than the insect. Readers can admire the cleverness while also noticing how the speaker bends logic to suit his desire.\n\nDonne carried the same habits into his religious poetry. In a sonnet that begins \"Death, be not proud,\" the speaker addresses death directly, as if it were an arrogant person, and argues it out of its power, insisting that death is merely a short sleep before eternal waking. In another sonnet, he asks God to batter his heart, using images of a besieged town and a captive to describe a soul that can be freed only by force. These poems are dramatic arguments, full of sudden turns, questions and commands. The speaker never simply describes a feeling; he wrestles with it.\n\nNot everyone has admired this style. In 1779, the critic Samuel Johnson complained that in poets like Donne, the most unlike ideas were \"yoked by violence together.\" Johnson meant it as criticism, and for more than a century Donne was regarded as a curiosity. In the early twentieth century, however, poets and critics, including T. S. Eliot, praised Donne for uniting thought and feeling, and his reputation rose sharply. The very quality Johnson disliked, the violent joining of distant ideas, came to be seen as his genius.\n\nReading Donne well requires patience with his difficulty. His sentences twist, his logic doubles back and his comparisons seem at first to make no sense. The reward comes when a strange image suddenly clicks into place and reveals something a conventional comparison could never show.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the central idea of the passage?","options":["Donne's surprising conceits force readers to think and reveal new meanings.","Donne wrote mainly simple love poems about roses, stars and summer days.","Donne's poems were praised by every critic from his time to the present."],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"As used in the passage, what is a \"conceit\"?","options":["An extended comparison between two very unlike things","A feeling of pride in one's own clever writing","A short poem written for a religious ceremony"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The feature that most divides those readers is the conceit, an extended metaphor that links two things so unlike each other that the comparison at first seems absurd."},{"type":"inference","q":"In the compass poem, what does the fixed foot's steadiness represent?","options":["The beloved's faithfulness, which keeps the speaker connected","The speaker's fear that he will never return from his journey","The couple's plan to move to a new city together"],"answer":0},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author include Samuel Johnson's complaint?","options":["To show how the same quality was first criticized and later praised","To prove that Donne's poems should no longer be taught in schools","To explain why Donne decided to stop writing love poetry"],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":658,"auto":{"fk":8.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u5-c01","unit":"G12-U5","pool":"check","title":"Hopkins and the Shape of Things","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Gerard Manley Hopkins is one of the few major poets whose work was almost entirely unknown while he lived. Born in England in 1844, he studied classics at Oxford, converted to Roman Catholicism and in 1868 entered the Jesuit order, a decision that led him to burn many of his early poems. For several years he wrote almost nothing, believing that poetry might conflict with his religious vocation. When he returned to writing, his poems were so unusual that the few editors who saw them refused to print them. He died in Dublin in 1889, at forty-four, having published almost nothing. His friend Robert Bridges, who had kept copies of his work, finally brought out a collection in 1918, nearly thirty years later. Readers were astonished, and Hopkins was soon recognized as one of the most original voices of the Victorian age.\n\nMuch of that originality grew from an idea Hopkins called inscape. He believed that every created thing, whether a bluebell, a falcon, a cloud or a chestnut tree, possessed a distinctive inner pattern that made it uniquely itself. The poet's task was to perceive that pattern and to express it precisely. A related word, instress, described the force that held a thing's inscape together and the impression that inscape made on an attentive observer. For Hopkins, a deeply religious man, these patterns were signs of the divine, so noticing the particular shape of a leaf or a wave was itself a kind of praise.\n\nThis conviction shaped his language. Because he wanted to capture the exact character of things, Hopkins piled up adjectives, coined new compound words and joined ideas with hyphens. He was fascinated by the sounds of Old English and Welsh poetry, and he filled his lines with alliteration, internal rhyme and repeated consonants. The effect is dense, energetic and sometimes difficult. His poems often feel less like descriptions than like attempts to make the reader experience the thing directly, through the mouth and ear as well as the mind.\n\nThe most famous technical feature of his poetry is what he called sprung rhythm. Most English verse of his time was organized by counting syllables in regular patterns, usually alternating unstressed and stressed beats. Hopkins instead counted only the stressed syllables in each line and allowed the number of unstressed syllables between them to vary. A line might contain two unstressed syllables in a row, or three, or none at all, so that heavy stresses could collide. Hopkins argued that this method was closer to natural speech and to the rhythms of music. In practice, it gave his poems a surging, irregular pulse that seems to accelerate and halt as the subject demands.\n\nHis short poem \"Pied Beauty\" shows these ideas at work. It begins with the line \"Glory be to God for dappled things,\" and then celebrates everything that is spotted, streaked or mixed: speckled skies, the markings of trout, fallen chestnuts, the patchwork of fields and the tools of different trades. The poem praises variety itself, the countless ways in which the world refuses to be uniform. Its form fits its subject, because it is a curtal sonnet, a shortened version of the sonnet that Hopkins invented. Like the dappled things it celebrates, the poem is familiar in pattern but unexpected in proportion.\n\nHopkins also wrote darker poems. In the last years of his life, while teaching in Dublin, he suffered periods of exhaustion and depression, and he wrote a group of sonnets that critics call the terrible sonnets. In these poems, the energetic praise of his earlier work gives way to anguish, doubt and a struggle to feel the presence of God. Yet they show the same compressed language and the same refusal to soften difficult experience.\n\nReading Hopkins aloud is often the best way to understand him. His rhythms, coined words and clustered sounds are meant to be heard. When they are, the difficulty of the page turns into something closer to music, and the reader begins to sense what Hopkins hoped to show: that each thing in the world has a shape worth noticing.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?","options":["Hopkins shaped new forms and sounds to capture what made each thing unique.","Hopkins wrote traditional verse that was popular with readers in his lifetime.","Hopkins gave up poetry entirely after he joined the Jesuit religious order."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Hopkins wanted readers to experience things through sound?","options":["His poems often feel less like descriptions than like attempts to make the reader experience the thing directly, through the mouth and ear as well as the mind.","His friend Robert Bridges, who had kept copies of his work, finally brought out a collection in 1918, nearly thirty years later.","In the last years of his life, while teaching in Dublin, he suffered periods of exhaustion and depression, and he wrote a group of sonnets that critics call the terrible sonnets."],"answer":0,"evidence":"His poems often feel less like descriptions than like attempts to make the reader experience the thing directly, through the mouth and ear as well as the mind."},{"type":"vocab","q":"As used in the passage, what does \"inscape\" mean?","options":["The distinctive inner pattern that makes a thing itself","A landscape painting that hangs inside a church","The space left blank at the end of a poem's line"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He believed that every created thing, whether a bluebell, a falcon, a cloud or a chestnut tree, possessed a distinctive inner pattern that made it uniquely itself."},{"type":"craft","q":"How does sprung rhythm differ from most English verse of Hopkins's time?","options":["It counts stresses and lets the unstressed syllables vary.","It counts every syllable and keeps the pattern perfectly even.","It removes all stressed syllables so the lines sound flat."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the author say the form of \"Pied Beauty\" fits its subject?","options":["A shortened sonnet, like dappled things, is familiar yet unexpected.","A long sonnet allows space to list every animal in the countryside.","A sonnet was the only form that editors of his time would publish."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":684,"auto":{"fk":9.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u5-c02","unit":"G12-U5","pool":"check","title":"Letters to a Glacier","genre":"poetry","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## First Letter: Arrival\n\nDear glacier, I came because my teacher said\nyou would not be here when my children were old enough to come.\nI expected something like a postcard:\nblue and enormous, a white wall breathing cold.\nInstead I found you gray at the edges,\nstreaked with gravel, slumped against the mountain\nlike a tired animal that has lain down to rest\nand has not yet decided whether it will get up.\nThe guide said you once filled this valley to the rim.\nShe pointed to a line of scraped rock, high above us,\nthe way a mother points to marks on a doorframe.\nThat is how tall you were, she said. That is how tall.\n\n## Second Letter: Sound\n\nNo one told me you would be loud.\nAll afternoon you ticked and groaned,\nand once, somewhere inside you, a crack went off\nlike a door slammed in a house where someone is grieving.\nMeltwater ran under the ice in a hidden river,\nand I lay on a flat rock and listened to it,\na sound like a crowd applauding very far away\nfor something that had already ended.\nI have been taught that silence is the sign of death.\nYou taught me the opposite.\nYour dying is the noisiest thing on the mountain.\n\n## Third Letter: Arithmetic\n\nAt school we learned you as a graph,\na line descending from left to right,\nneat as a staircase someone is walking down.\nWe learned the words retreat and mass balance,\nwhich sound like strategy, like a careful budget,\nas if somewhere a patient accountant were keeping track.\nBut standing here, I cannot see the graph.\nI see a boulder you carried for a thousand years\nset down at last in a meadow, like luggage left on a platform.\nI see small flowers growing where you were,\nbright and ordinary, not knowing what they replaced.\nThe numbers were true. They were not enough.\n\n## Fourth Letter: Names\n\nThe guide told me the old people of the valley had a name for you\nthat meant something like the grandmother who keeps the water.\nIn spring you let the rivers go, a little at a time,\nso the fields below could drink all summer long.\nNow you give too much too quickly, like a fortune spent in a single season,\nand the farmers who once trusted your slowness\nwatch the river rise in June and wonder about August.\nI wrote your name in my notebook, carefully, letter by letter,\nthe way you copy an address you are afraid of losing.\n\n## Fifth Letter: Confession\n\nI flew to see you. I should tell you that.\nThe plane burned its share of the very air\nthat is taking you apart, and I knew this,\nand I came anyway, because I wanted to stand here\nand say that I had seen you while there was something to see.\nI am not sure whether that makes me a witness or a mourner\nor one more visitor who took a photograph and left.\nPerhaps it makes me all three.\nI promise to carry this unease home with me\nand not to set it down in the first comfortable place,\nthe way you finally set down your boulder.\n\n## Last Letter: Leaving\n\nWhen I turned at the bend of the trail to look back,\nthe light was going, and for a moment you were blue again,\nthe old blue of the postcards, lit from inside.\nI understand it was only the angle of the sun.\nI understand that the blue was the color of ice\nso dense it swallows every other color.\nStill, I took it as a kind of answer,\nthe way you might take a wave from someone across a station\nwho may only have been adjusting a scarf.\nDear glacier, I will tell my children what you sounded like.\nI will tell them how tall you were.\nI will show them the line on the rock\nand say, That is how tall. That is how tall.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"How does the poem's structure as a series of letters shape its effect?","options":["It makes the glacier a listener the speaker addresses directly.","It turns the poem into a scientific report on glacier loss.","It lets the glacier answer each of the speaker's questions."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that data alone was insufficient to convey the loss?","options":["At school we learned you as a graph, a line descending from left to right, neat as a staircase someone is walking down.","The numbers were true.","They were not enough."],"answer":2,"evidence":"They were not enough."},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of comparing the scraped line of rock to marks on a doorframe?","options":["It makes the glacier's loss feel like a family's measure of time.","It suggests the glacier was once small and is now growing.","It shows that the guide has children who live nearby."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the speaker wonder whether the trip makes the speaker \"a witness or a mourner\" or a visitor?","options":["The speaker is unsure whether the trip honors or harms the glacier.","The speaker plans to become a guide and lead tours on the mountain.","The speaker believes the glacier will recover before the next visit."],"answer":0},{"type":"theme","q":"What does the repeated line \"That is how tall\" suggest by the end of the poem?","options":["Memory and testimony will carry what the glacier cannot.","The speaker has measured the glacier with great precision.","The glacier is growing taller again every winter."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":665,"auto":{"fk":6.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-u5-c03","unit":"G12-U5","pool":"check","title":"The Last Two Hundred","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The gun goes off, and for the first time in four years Mireille does not hear it as a sound but feels it as a push between her shoulder blades, as though someone she trusts has placed a hand there and said, now. The track is still wet from the afternoon rain, and the stadium lights turn every puddle in the outer lanes into a small white coin. She is in lane four; she has always been in lane four, or so it seems tonight, as if her whole life has been a series of ovals she was assigned to run.\n\nThe first hundred meters belong to the body. Her coach used to say that, back when Mireille was fourteen and thought running was something you decided to do with your mind. The body knows the curve, he said; let it lean. So she leans, and the curve receives her the way a river receives a boat, and the girl in lane five, a tall runner from the coastal school with braids tied in red thread, drifts slightly ahead, the red flickering at the edge of Mireille's vision like a match someone keeps lighting and shaking out.\n\nHer mother is in the stands. Mireille does not look, because looking costs time, but she knows exactly where her mother is sitting: third row from the top, near the aisle, wearing the green raincoat that is too large for her because it once belonged to Mireille's grandmother. Her mother has never understood running. She worked double shifts at the hospital for most of Mireille's childhood and came to perhaps one race a season, always late, always breathless, always apologizing as though she had been the one competing. Tonight she arrived an hour early. Mireille saw the green coat climbing the steps during warm-ups and had to turn away so that no one would see her face.\n\nThe second hundred belongs to the mind. This is where the burning starts, a slow ache in the thighs that climbs toward the lungs, and the mind begins its bargaining. You could ease off; you have already qualified; no one would blame you. Mireille has heard this voice so many times that she has given it a name, the Accountant, because it is always reasonable and always counting what things cost. She does not argue with it anymore, because arguing is a conversation, and conversations take breath. She simply lets it talk, the way you might let a radio play in another room.\n\nThe red thread is a full stride ahead now. The straightaway opens before them, and the finish line is a white stripe that seems, as it always does, to be moving slightly away.\n\nWhen Mireille was nine, she ran for the first time without being told to. It was on a beach, early in the morning, and her mother was asleep on a towel after a night shift, and the sand was cool and hard near the water. She ran until the beach curved out of sight and then ran back, and her mother was awake and sitting up with her hand shading her eyes, and she said, Where did you go? And Mireille said, Nowhere. I just went. Her mother laughed and did not ask any more questions, and Mireille has always remembered that, the way she was allowed to have gone nowhere and come back without explaining.\n\nThe last fifty meters belong to neither the body nor the mind. Mireille has never found the right word for what they belong to. Her arms are heavy, her stride has shortened, and the Accountant has gone quiet, as if even it knows that the bill has already been paid. The red thread is close again, close enough that Mireille can hear the other girl's breathing, ragged and determined, exactly like her own.\n\nThey reach the line together. The scoreboard will decide, in a moment, which of them was first by a hundredth of a second, and the decision will matter to the newspapers and the scouts and the coaches with clipboards along the fence.\n\nBut Mireille is already looking up, past the lights, to the third row from the top, near the aisle, where a woman in a green coat that is too large for her is standing with one hand raised to shade her eyes, though there is no sun, and the night is falling on the stadium like a quiet blue cloth.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"What is the main effect of telling the story in the present tense?","options":["It makes the race feel immediate, as if happening now.","It shows that the race took place many years earlier.","It hides the fact that Mireille is narrating her own story."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does the author interrupt the final straightaway with the memory of the beach?","options":["To slow the climax and reveal what running means to Mireille","To explain the rules of the race to readers who do not know them","To show that Mireille would rather be at the beach than racing"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that the race result matters less to Mireille than her mother does?","options":["They reach the line together.","The red thread is a full stride ahead now.","Her mother is in the stands."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Her mother is in the stands."},{"type":"craft","q":"How does the final image connect to the earlier memory?","options":["The mother shades her eyes again, linking tonight to the beach.","The mother leaves early again, repeating her old habit.","The mother wears new clothes, showing she has changed."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"What does the name \"the Accountant\" suggest about Mireille's inner voice?","options":["It weighs costs and urges her to protect herself.","It cheers her on and demands that she run faster.","It worries about the money her family has spent."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":742,"auto":{"fk":8.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-book-ch1","unit":"G12-U2","pool":"book","book":"The Home Quarter","chapter":1,"title":"The Home Quarter, Part 1: Moisture","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The wheat was ready on the twenty-sixth of June, which Tessa Halvorsen knew before anyone told her, because she had been rubbing heads of grain between her palms every evening for a week. When the kernels came loose cleanly and cracked between her teeth instead of bending, she walked back to the house and announced it at supper. Her grandmother, Ruth, who had owned the farm for fifty-one years, chewed a kernel of her own, nodded once and said they would start after the dew burned off.\n\nThe Halvorsen place lay in north-central Kansas, eleven hundred acres of rolling ground divided by section roads that ran as straight as rulers toward a horizon that never seemed to get any closer. Most of it was planted in winter wheat, sown in the fall, dormant under the snow and green again by March. By late June the fields had turned the color of a lion's back, and the whole family's year came down to about two weeks of work, provided the weather cooperated and the machinery held.\n\nTessa was seventeen and had driven the grain cart since she was thirteen. Her job was to pull alongside the combine as it moved through the field, match its speed exactly, and catch the stream of grain pouring from its unloading auger without spilling a bushel. Then she hauled the cart to the edge of the field and emptied it into the semitrailer her father would drive to the elevator in town. It was a job that required patience, precise steering and a willingness to sit in a cab for fourteen hours while dust coated everything, including her teeth. She loved it in a way she had never managed to explain to her friends.\n\nHer father, Mark, operated the combine, while her mother, Lucía, a nurse at the county hospital, took vacation days every harvest to run parts, bring meals to the field and keep the books, which she described as the least romantic and most important job on the farm. Her grandmother, at seventy-eight, no longer drove any machinery, but she still rode out to the field each afternoon in her old pickup and sat with the window down, watching the combine's progress the way some people watch a ball game.\n\nThe first morning, Mark sent a sample of the grain to the elevator to test its moisture. The reading came back at just under thirteen and a half percent, dry enough to store safely, and he grinned at Tessa through the window of the combine as if he personally had arranged it. By noon the machines were running, and the field was full of the sounds she associated with the best part of the year: the rumble of the combine, the rattle of the cart, the hiss of grain sliding into the trailer.\n\nAt sunset, they stopped to eat in the shade of the semitrailer. Lucía had brought burritos wrapped in aluminum foil and a cooler of iced tea, which she distributed with the efficiency of someone accustomed to hospital shifts. Tessa sat on the tailgate with her boots dangling, too exhausted to talk and too contented to want to.\n\nIt was Ruth who eventually broke the silence, removing an envelope from the pocket of her jacket and laying it on the cooler lid, where she smoothed it deliberately with one weathered hand.\n\n\"I want everybody to hear this before somebody hears it at church,\" she said. \"A renewable energy company from Denver wants to lease the north section for a solar farm for thirty years, and they've made an offer that is, frankly, a generous one. I haven't said yes, and I haven't said no either.\"\n\nTessa looked at her father, who was studying his burrito as though it contained instructions. Her mother looked at Ruth with an expression that meant she had already known.\n\n\"The north section,\" Tessa said slowly, performing the calculation she had known since childhood. \"That's six hundred and forty acres.\"\n\n\"It is,\" Ruth said, \"and it's some of the most productive ground we own.\"\n\n\"We'd stop farming it completely?\"\n\n\"For thirty years, they'd have panels on it, and after that, they'd remove them and the land would return to the family.\" Ruth folded the envelope and returned it to her pocket. \"I'll be a hundred and eight by then, so I don't expect to be consulted.\"\n\nNobody laughed. A solitary meadowlark called from the fence line, and the combine ticked as its engine cooled. Tessa looked out at the north section, where the wheat stood waiting in the long gold light, and realized she was already trying to memorize it.\n\n\"We'll discuss it properly after harvest,\" her father said finally, \"because right now, we cut.\"\n\nSo they cut, but for the rest of that evening, every time Tessa pulled the cart alongside the combine, she found herself counting rows, as if she could calculate precisely how many remained.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"How did Tessa know the wheat was ready to harvest?","options":["She had been rubbing heads of grain between her palms.","Her father sent a grain sample to the elevator for a test.","Her grandmother saw the color of the fields from the house."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The wheat was ready on the twenty-sixth of June, which Tessa Halvorsen knew before anyone told her, because she had been rubbing heads of grain between her palms every evening for a week."},{"type":"craft","q":"What does the comparison of the fields to \"a lion's back\" emphasize?","options":["The rich golden color and strength of ripe wheat","The danger that wild animals pose to farm families","The way the wind flattens the wheat before harvest"],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Lucía's expression suggest when Ruth announces the offer?","options":["She had already learned about the solar lease.","She was shocked to hear about the company.","She thought Ruth was making a joke at supper."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Tessa find herself \"counting rows\" at the end of the chapter?","options":["She fears this may be the last harvest on the north section.","Her father asked her to measure how much wheat remained.","She is trying to finish early so she can meet her friends."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":825,"auto":{"fk":9.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-book-ch2","unit":"G12-U2","pool":"book","book":"The Home Quarter","chapter":2,"title":"The Home Quarter, Part 2: The Offer","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Harvest ended on the eighth of July, two days ahead of a line of thunderstorms that flattened a neighbor's late field and left the Halvorsens' stubble glistening and safe. That night the family sat at the kitchen table with the envelope open between them, along with a calculator, a pot of coffee and the farm's account books, which Lucía had carried in from the office like a pile of evidence.\n\nThe offer was simple to describe and difficult to think about. The company would pay a little over a thousand dollars per acre every year for the north section, with the payment rising slowly over thirty years. It would install rows of solar panels on steel posts, run cables underground to a substation near the highway and sell the electricity to a utility in Missouri. At the end of the lease, or if the project failed, the company would be required to remove the equipment and restore the land, and it would post a bond to guarantee the cost.\n\nLucía did the arithmetic out loud, entering each figure into the calculator with deliberate care: six hundred and forty acres at that rate came to roughly the same amount each year that the entire farm had earned, after expenses, in its three best years combined.\n\n\"In a good year,\" Mark said.\n\n\"In a good year,\" Lucía agreed, \"and we've endured two terrible ones in a row.\"\n\nTessa knew about the terrible years, because a drought had cut yields by nearly half two summers ago, and last year the price of wheat had fallen while the price of fertilizer, fuel and seed had not. The farm carried a loan on the newer combine, and another on equipment Mark had bought to expand into soybeans on the low ground. She had heard her parents talking about it late at night, their voices too quiet to make out the words but not quiet enough to hide the worry.\n\n\"So we simply stop being farmers on our best section,\" Tessa said.\n\n\"We'd still farm the remaining four hundred and sixty acres,\" her father said.\n\n\"That's not the same thing at all.\"\n\n\"No,\" Ruth said quietly, \"it isn't.\"\n\nTessa had expected her grandmother to defend the offer; instead, Ruth set down her coffee and told a story Tessa had never heard. In 1957, when Ruth was a young bride, her husband's family had nearly lost the farm. The drought of the early fifties had drained their savings, and the bank had been ready to call the loan. Her father-in-law sold a quarter section to a neighbor to keep the rest, and he never drove past that ground again without looking away.\n\n\"He said it was like selling a finger to save a hand,\" Ruth said. \"The hand survived, but he missed the finger every day of his life.\"\n\n\"Then why would you even consider signing this?\" Tessa asked.\n\n\"Because this time, nobody's selling anything, and the ground remains ours,\" Ruth said, tapping the papers. \"And because I'm seventy-eight, and your father has a back that hurts every morning, and I've watched him lie awake over prices that nobody in this kitchen can control. Wheat is a beautiful crop, Tessa, but it is also a gamble you make every single year with your whole life.\"\n\nThe next afternoon, Tessa drove to the grain elevator to pick up the final settlement check and ran into Dale Pruitt, who farmed the land east of theirs. Dale was a broad, sunburned man who served as a deacon at the Lutheran church and had coached Tessa's summer softball team for three seasons. He had already heard about the offer, as apparently everyone in the county had.\n\n\"You know what they're calling it at the coffee shop?\" he said, shaking his head. \"The glass farm. I'm not telling Ruth her business, but that's prime agricultural ground. My granddad always said God isn't making any more of it, and now we're covering it up to run air conditioners in Kansas City.\"\n\n\"It would come back after thirty years,\" Tessa said, surprised to hear herself defending the idea.\n\n\"Maybe,\" Dale said, \"and maybe once one section goes, the next one goes easier, since there's already talk of a second project down the road. I don't want to look out my kitchen window at a mile of panels.\"\n\nDriving home, Tessa realized that she agreed with nearly everything Dale had said and that she still could not dismiss anything her grandmother had said either. It was an uncomfortable feeling, like trying to stand on two sides of a fence at once. She had always assumed that being certain was the same as being right. For the first time, she began to suspect that some decisions offered no answer that would not cost somebody something.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What would the company be required to do when the lease ended?","options":["Remove the equipment and restore the land","Buy the north section from the Halvorsens","Plant soybeans across the entire section"],"answer":0,"evidence":"At the end of the lease, or if the project failed, the company would be required to remove the equipment and restore the land, and it would post a bond to guarantee the cost."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Ruth tell the story about her father-in-law selling land?","options":["To show she understands loss but sees the lease as different","To prove that the family should sell the north section quickly","To explain why she has always disliked their neighbor Dale"],"answer":0},{"type":"compare","q":"How does Dale's view of the offer differ from Ruth's?","options":["Dale stresses losing farmland, while Ruth stresses the family's money risks.","Dale wants to lease his own land, while Ruth refuses to consider any lease.","Dale cares about wheat prices, while Ruth cares only about the view."],"answer":0},{"type":"theme","q":"Which idea does the final paragraph introduce?","options":["Some hard choices have costs no matter which side is chosen.","Neighbors should always agree before making family decisions.","Being certain about a decision proves that it is the right one."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":802,"auto":{"fk":8.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-book-ch3","unit":"G12-U2","pool":"book","book":"The Home Quarter","chapter":3,"title":"The Home Quarter, Part 3: Under the Panels","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In late July, Ruth asked Tessa to drive her to see a solar farm. The nearest one that had been running for several years was two hours south, outside a town Tessa had passed through only on the way to a basketball tournament. Ruth wore her church blouse and carried a spiral notebook, while Tessa brought a skeptical attitude and a thermos of coffee, and she did not expect either one to change.\n\nThe site manager who met them at the gate was a woman named Grace Okafor, who looked about thirty and wore steel-toed boots and a wide-brimmed hat. She had grown up on a cattle ranch in western Nebraska, she explained as she unlocked a gate, and she had studied electrical engineering because she liked machines that worked without anyone having to feed them at five in the morning.\n\nThe panels stood in long, low rows that followed the gentle slope of the ground, each one tilting slowly through the day to track the sun. Tessa had imagined a glittering sheet of glass covering the earth. Instead, she saw grass, because between and beneath the rows grew a thick mix of native prairie plants, little bluestem and coneflowers and milkweed, alive with bees and butterflies. In the shade under one row, a flock of sheep lay chewing with the bored contentment of animals who have found the best spot in the county.\n\n\"A local rancher grazes them here under contract,\" Grace said, \"because they keep the vegetation down, so we don't have to mow, and he receives free pasture, which means everyone benefits except the mower.\"\n\nRuth wrote that down carefully.\n\nTessa knelt and pushed her fingers into the soil beneath a panel. It was cool and remarkably dark, darker than she had anticipated. Grace explained that because the ground had not been plowed for years, the roots of the perennial grasses had begun rebuilding organic matter, and soil scientists from the state university were measuring the change. \"When the lease ends,\" she said, \"the land might be in better shape than when we arrived. That depends entirely on how it's managed, and not every site does it right.\"\n\n\"So it's not entirely positive,\" Tessa said.\n\nGrace considered her for a moment, as if deciding how honest to be. \"Nothing is entirely positive, and the panels need replacing eventually, and recycling them is still harder than it should be. Some projects are built on the best farmland when poorer ground nearby would work just as well. And some neighbors resent us,\" she added with a shrug. \"A few of them still won't wave at me, and honestly, I understand it, because this used to be wheat.\"\n\nOn the drive home, Ruth was quiet for a long time. Then she asked Tessa what she wanted to do with her life.\n\nIt was a question Tessa had been avoiding even in her own head. She had always assumed she would farm, but her guidance counselor had been pushing her toward the agronomy program at the state university, and lately she had begun reading about soil carbon, drought-resistant wheat varieties and the ways farmers were adapting to hotter summers. She had started to see that loving the land might mean understanding it, and that understanding it might take her away for a while.\n\n\"I want to farm,\" she said finally, \"but I want to understand what I'm doing, not just the way Dad does it or the way Grandpa did it, because I want to know why it works.\"\n\nRuth nodded slowly. \"Your grandfather would have appreciated that answer. He used to read seed catalogs the way other men read novels.\"\n\nThe county commission held a public meeting on the second solar project the following week, and the Halvorsens went to listen. The meeting room in the courthouse annex was crowded beyond capacity. Dale Pruitt spoke about prime farmland and declining property values, and a retired teacher spoke about climate change and the future her grandchildren would inherit. A young farmer named Luis Herrera said the lease payments would let him keep farming at all, since his family's land could not support two households anymore. A woman Tessa did not know said that she simply did not want to see panels from her porch, and that her feelings deserved to count too.\n\nTessa listened to every speaker and found that she could not dismiss any of them. When the meeting ended, she realized that what bothered her was not the disagreement. It was how certain each person sounded, and how little anyone seemed to be listening to the others.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Tessa do just after Grace explained the sheep grazing?","options":["She knelt and pushed her fingers into the soil beneath a panel.","She asked Grace whether she had grown up on a cattle ranch.","She spoke at the county commission meeting about prime farmland."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Tessa knelt and pushed her fingers into the soil beneath a panel."},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Grace's honest answer about problems suggest about her character?","options":["She values truth over simply selling the project.","She secretly wants the solar farm to be removed.","She is unsure how the panels actually work."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"How does the visit to the solar farm change Tessa's expectations?","options":["She expects glass covering the earth but finds grass and animals.","She expects sheep and prairie plants but finds bare dirt instead.","She expects friendly neighbors but finds that everyone is angry."],"answer":0},{"type":"theme","q":"What does the county meeting reveal about the community?","options":["Each side holds real concerns but few people listen to others.","Most residents agree that solar farms should be banned outright.","The commission has already decided the outcome before the meeting."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":771,"auto":{"fk":8.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g12-book-ch4","unit":"G12-U2","pool":"book","book":"The Home Quarter","chapter":4,"title":"The Home Quarter, Part 4: The Home Quarter","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The family gathered at the kitchen table again on the first evening in August. The envelope had grown into a folder thick with documents: the lease, a lawyer's comments, a letter from the county, and Ruth's notebook, now filled with questions written in her small, slanting hand. Outside, the stubble fields had begun to turn pale and brittle, and the cicadas were so loud that Lucía closed the window.\n\nRuth spoke first, explaining that she had asked the company for numerous changes and that its representatives had agreed to most of them. The lease would require native grasses under the panels and allow sheep grazing. The company would pay for soil testing at the beginning and the end of the project, and the restoration bond would be larger than the original offer. The panels would be set back farther from Dale Pruitt's property line, behind a row of planted cedars. She had also asked them to move the access road so it would not cut across the old creek bottom where her husband had proposed to her, though she said that this request had been, strictly speaking, sentimental.\n\n\"I'm going to sign it,\" she said, \"but only for the north section.\"\n\nTessa had known this was coming for weeks, and it still landed in her chest like a dropped weight. She looked down at the table and waited until she could trust her voice.\n\n\"There's something else,\" Ruth went on, \"concerning the home quarter, the hundred and sixty acres surrounding this house. I'm putting it in a trust for you, Tessa. It isn't the whole farm, only that particular ground, and your parents will keep farming the rest with the lease money as a cushion. But the home quarter will be yours to manage when you finish school, if you still want it. You can grow wheat on it the way we always have, or try those drought varieties you keep reading about, or anything else you think the land can bear. It will be your experiment.\"\n\nFor a moment, nobody spoke; Mark rubbed his eyes with the heel of his hand, and Lucía reached over and squeezed Tessa's wrist.\n\n\"I don't know what to say,\" Tessa said.\n\n\"Then say you'll go to college,\" Ruth said, \"and come back knowing considerably more than I do, which shouldn't be particularly difficult.\"\n\nLater that evening, Tessa walked to the edge of the north section. The sun was low, and the stubble glowed copper in the angled light. She thought about the meeting at the courthouse, about Dale's anger and Luis Herrera's worry and the woman who did not want to see panels from her porch. Every one of them had been defending something genuine, and so had her grandmother. The difference, Tessa decided, was that Ruth had listened before she chose, and had tried to protect as many of those real things as she could, even the ones that belonged to other people.\n\nThe next morning, Tessa drove to Dale Pruitt's farm and found him in his machine shed, replacing a belt on a swather. She told him about the setback and the cedars before he could hear it at the coffee shop, and she told him about the soil testing and the sheep.\n\nDale wiped his hands on a rag for a long time. \"I still don't like it,\" he said eventually.\n\n\"I know you don't.\"\n\n\"But I appreciate you telling me straight, without making me hear it secondhand.\" He looked out the open door of the shed toward the north section. \"Ruth always did things properly, so tell her the cedars better be the good kind, because eastern red cedar spreads like a rumor.\"\n\nTessa laughed for the first time in several days.\n\nIn September, she submitted her application to the agronomy program, and in October, she helped her father plant winter wheat on the home quarter, watching the drill press seeds into the dark ground in long, even lines. The seeds would lie dormant through the winter, under frost and snow, waiting for spring. She knew she would be away at school when they turned green again, and that she would miss the next harvest, and perhaps the one after that.\n\nBut she also knew what would be waiting when she came back. On the last evening of planting, she stood at the edge of the field and rubbed a handful of soil between her palms, the way she had once rubbed heads of grain, as if testing whether the future was ready.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What did Ruth decide to do with the home quarter?","options":["Put it in a trust for Tessa","Lease it to the solar company","Sell it to Dale Pruitt"],"answer":0,"evidence":"I'm putting it in a trust for you, Tessa."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Tessa visit Dale Pruitt the morning after the family meeting?","options":["To tell him the changes in person before he hears rumors","To ask him to sign the solar lease along with her family","To borrow his swather for planting the home quarter"],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"How does the final image connect to the opening of the book?","options":["Tessa tests the soil the way she once tested ripe wheat, now for the future.","Tessa gives up farming the way her grandfather gave up a quarter section.","Tessa watches a storm the way her family raced storms during harvest."],"answer":0},{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does the book develop most fully by the end?","options":["Wise choices weigh many real interests rather than seeking a perfect answer.","Old traditions should always be protected from any kind of change.","Young people should leave farming and move to cities for better jobs."],"answer":0}],"grade":12,"words":754,"auto":{"fk":7.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}}];
